/-!
# Regular sequences in a quotient of a flat local extension: the equivalence

Let `R → S` be a flat local homomorphism of Noetherian local rings whose closed fibre
`S / 𝔪_R S` is a regular local ring, let `I ⊆ 𝔪_R` be an ideal of `R` and let `J ⊆ S` be an ideal
containing `I S`. The following are equivalent: `J` is generated by a regular sequence and `S / J`
has finite Tor dimension over `R / I`; the same regular-sequence condition together with eventual
vanishing of `Tor` against the residue field of `R` alone; and regular-sequence generation of both
`I` and `J / IS`.

The implication (3) ⇒ (1) is
`Stacks.CompleteIntersection.isGeneratedByRegularSequence_and_finiteTorDimension` and needs no
regularity of the closed fibre. The implication (1) ⇒ (2) is a weakening of the test module. The
remaining implication (2) ⇒ (3) is where the closed fibre enters: eventual vanishing of `Tor`
against the residue field says that the derived fibre of `S / J` over `R / I` is concentrated in a
window, so `S / J` is perfect over `S / IS` by the descent criterion
`StacksDraft.Perfect.isPerfect_and_torAmplitude_of_flat_local_of_regular_closedFibre`, hence has
finite projective dimension there, and the absolute statement [Stacks, Tag 09PW] applies to
`I S ⊆ J` inside `S`. That gives a regular sequence generating `I S`, which descends to one
generating `I` because Koszul-regular ideals descend along a faithfully flat map, and a regular
sequence generating `J / IS`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.CompleteIntersection.regularSequence_quotient_tfae_of_flat`: the equivalence.

## Implementation notes

An ideal of a local ring generated by a regular sequence is Koszul-regular, and conversely a
Koszul-regular proper ideal of a Noetherian local ring is generated by a regular sequence: at the
closed point the local generators live over a principal localization away from a unit, which is
the ring itself. This round trip through `Ideal.IsKoszulRegular` is how the faithfully flat descent
of [Stacks, Tag 068N] is applied to a statement phrased with explicit regular sequences.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 09PX](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09PX)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 09PW](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09PW)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 09PC](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09PC)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 068N](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/068N)
-/

@[expose] public section

